\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Couplings and weights under inversion and conjugation}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{The inversion and charge maps of \ref{fdip-013T} act on graded
spin fields. Their action on spin directions and links is described
in [symmetries of graded spin fields](fdip-0170). For orthogonal sign
spaces with unitary group actions, an isometric exchange exists
exactly when both sign spaces admit bijective intertwiners with their
opposites at the target, both linear or both conjugate-linear for the
same change of group (\ref{fdip-018D}). Which specified inversions
and charge conjugations realize these exchanges and transport the
even onsite coefficient (\ref{fdip-018H}, \ref{fdip-018I})?}

\p{On the identified field space of the [spinor time map](fdip-017Z),
use a self-adjoint spin grading anticommuting with every direction
and a sitewise charge conjugation commuting with those directions.
Use the inverse of that charge conjugation in the time map. Applying
spatial inversion, charge conjugation and the time map in that order
gives unitary graded total inversion (\ref{fdip-019E}). For represented
unitary links, positive spacing and real mass and Wilson coefficient,
it transports the massive Wilson kernel to the inverted links and
preserves its determinant (\ref{fdip-019F}).}

\p{The real-trace plaquette sum gives an invariant gauge factor when
composed with any real function (\ref{fdip-019H}). For the
[connection half-algebras](fdip-019L), suppose the site time reflection
and total inversion preserve the connection probability by measurable
oriented link pullback. Total inversion then carries the reflected
pairing from one closed half to the other (\ref{fdip-019N}).}

\transclude{fdip-0188}
\transclude{fdip-019A}
